HueyNexusController.body:
HueyNexusController restores and repurposes Google Nexus devices as dedicated physical control surfaces for Huey and Huey Body.

| Platform | Codename | Intended role |
|---|---|---|
| Google Nexus 5 | `hammerhead` | Canonical pocket-sized handset controller |
| Nexus 7 (2012 Wi-Fi) | `grouper` | Compact tablet controller and status display |
| Nexus 7 (2012 mobile) | `tilapia` | Mobile-connected tablet controller where hardware permits |
| Nexus 7 (2013 Wi-Fi) | `flo` | Higher-resolution tablet controller |
| Nexus 7 (2013 LTE) | `deb` | LTE-capable tablet controller where hardware permits |

“Supported” means formally maintained project targets. It does not mean every kernel path, modem, microphone, camera, sensor, charger, battery procedure, image, or controller feature has passed validation.

## Shared controller model

- dedicated rather than personal-use images;
- touchscreen and voice request surfaces;
- authenticated HIMS transport target;
- identical authority model across handset and tablet layouts;
- device-specific credentials and revocation;
- replaceable hardware that does not carry Huey's identity or canonical memory;
- recoverable images, controlled updates, rollback, repair, and known limitations.

A controller command is a request. It does not bypass authorization, confirmation, safe-stop, or Body execution gates.

Nexus-5-Hammerhead.body:
The Google Nexus 5 `hammerhead` is the canonical handset reference for HueyNexusController.

## Intended role

- pocketable one-handed status and acknowledgement;
- touchscreen command composition and review;
- recorded or live voice request capture where validated;
- reconnect, revoke, shutdown, recovery, and emergency request surfaces;
- portable operation near Huey Body.

## Acceptance evidence

A maintained image needs a documented bootloader and recovery path, checksums, repeatable installation, display and touch, Wi-Fi, USB, audio, charging and battery telemetry, suspend/wake/shutdown, thermal behavior, controller application launch, provisioning, HIMS authentication, revocation, and known limitations.

## Non-claims

The project does not presently claim complete native Debian hardware support, production-ready HIMS authentication, safe battery modification, or granted Body-control authority on `hammerhead`.

Controller-OS-and-Interface.body:
## Host operating system

| Layer | Direction |
|---|---|
| **Primary research target** | Native Debian-based system booting directly on supported Nexus hardware |
| **Practical fallback** | LineageOS when native Linux cannot provide reliable hardware support |
| **Preferred UI** | Phosh, GTK, Wayland, and a purpose-built PyHuey/Huey controller application |
| **Fallback UI** | KDE Plasma Mobile |

The controller protocol, provisioning, authority, logging, and replacement model should remain stable even if the host operating system changes.

## Minimum interface surfaces

- connection and authentication state;
- registered device identity;
- Huey and Body availability;
- command composition, review, and confirmation;
- voice-capture state where enabled;
- acknowledgements and final responses;
- alerts, refusal, and safe-state indicators;
- revoke, reconnect, shutdown, and recovery controls;
- local diagnostics, hardware profile, and build/version information.

The interface should be touch-first and device-aware rather than a complete desktop squeezed onto a small screen.

Controller-Security-and-Recovery.body:
## Security requirements

- documented image provenance and checksums;
- minimal installed software;
- device-specific provisioning and credentials;
- protected secret storage appropriate to the platform;
- explicit microphone, camera, sensor, and network permissions;
- authentication-failure and offline-safe behavior;
- replay resistance and fresh-message checks;
- controlled update and rollback;
- revocation and replacement of lost or damaged devices;
- logs without credentials or unnecessary audio;
- no personal phone or tablet data in the controller image.

## Replacement proof

A credible controller family must demonstrate that one approved device can be revoked and another provisioned without changing Huey's identity or losing audit attribution.

## Recovery package

Each release should identify the supported device, image checksum, bootloader/recovery prerequisites, installation steps, known limitations, provisioning process, rollback, data wipe, credential revocation, spare-device onboarding, and support end condition.

Controller-Battery-and-Repair.body:
Every controller battery requires model-specific evaluation. Age alone does not determine safety, and successful boot does not prove acceptable battery behavior.

## Inspect and measure

- swelling, puncture, corrosion, odor, heat, and enclosure deformation;
- open-circuit and loaded voltage stability;
- charge acceptance and cutoff behavior;
- temperature during charge and sustained screen/network/audio load;
- remaining capacity and unexpected shutdown;
- telemetry accuracy;
- connector, cable, adhesive, strain-relief, and physical-protection condition.

## Modification rule

A custom or larger battery is not acceptable merely because it fits. Charging, cell protection, temperature monitoring, current limits, mechanical protection, fire-risk mitigation, serviceability, and rollback must be documented and tested.

## Spare pool

Operational, development, recovery, backup, and parts-donor devices should be labeled separately. Preserve exact variant, condition, installed image, keys/revocation state, battery record, known-good parts, and last validation date.

LabTech.body:
**LabTech is external operator tooling.** Current named systems include the **2017 iMac 5K** and **Briefcase**.

## Permitted roles

- repository editing and review;
- building packages, images, and documentation;
- inspecting logs and artifacts;
- recovery media and controller reprovisioning;
- hardware documentation and diagnostics;
- backups and restore testing;
- bounded operator access to Huey.

## Boundary rules

LabTech systems do not carry Huey's identity merely because they operate the project. Their permissions should be bounded, attributable, revocable where possible, and separated from canonical memory or physical-control authority.

A LabTech machine may temporarily host a tool or build process without becoming part of the local node architecture. Diagrams should show this as a support relationship, not identity transfer.
